  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 259 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Surface high pressure builds into the region \ntoday, leading to mostly sunny skies, lighter winds and high \ntemperatures in the 50s for most places. Relatively widespread \nfrost is likely tomorrow morning, but winds are expected to \nremain brisk for a longer portion of the overnight, so extent of\nfrost formation in the Champlain valley is somewhat limited, \nthough it could be enough for a Frost Advisory to be issued \nlater. High pressure slides east Sunday and return southerly \nflow will provide warmer temperatures to rise into the 60s \nareawide. High clouds ahead of Isaias will infiltrate the region\nduring the afternoon Sunday. Widespread rainfall will hold off \nuntil after sunset for most locations with a quarter to half of \nan inch of rain expected for many locations. Areas near the \nCanadian Border will likely see the least rainfall with amounts \ncloser to a tenth of an inch. Will have to monitor for heavier \nbands within the precipitation shield. This will likely change \nrun to run and with the lack of mesoscale models having the \ntemporal resolution to analyze this feature, we won't know about\nplacement of this possible banded precipitation until tomorrow \nafternoon most likely. Nevertheless, the rainfall seen Sunday \nnight, even if it's in the 1-1.5 inch rain locally, will be \nhighly beneficial with no flood concerns anticipated at this \ntime. A strengthening mid-level jet Sunday night will help to \nquickly shift these remnants eastward with dry conditions \nexpected by daybreak on Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A surface low will pass through from west to east \nTuesday night, bringing widespread showers. While it will be quick \nmoving and technically more northern stream and not have a strong \nconnection to Gulf/Atlantic moisture, it will include the remnants \nof Tropical Storm Rachael so there will be some efficient \nrainfall with the former tropical moisture. GEFS/EPS/CMCE \ncombined probabilities of a half inch of rain range between \n30-50 percent and probabilities of an inch are below 10 percent,\nso the rain would only be beneficial. Warm air aloft will \nremain on the backside so even though the cold front drops \ntemperatures significantly, rainfall amounts on Sunday have \nincreased slightly with most locations seeing between a quarter \nto half of an inch of rain with closer to a tenth of an inch of \nrain near the Canadian Border.ny upslope showers look to remain \nrain in the mountains. Dry, sunny and seasonably cool conditions\ndevelop for Thursday before more shower chances arrive for \nFriday and the weekend.",
